<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamagata Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamagata Prefecture is a prefecture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku region</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, with the city of Yamagata as its capital. It was formed from the former Dewa Province and the domains of the area, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> and Shōnai, after the abolition of the han system in 1871, and took its present shape in 1876. It is known for fruit growing and the sacred mountains of Dewa Sanzan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture.jpg" alt="Yamagata Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: さかおり · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_twilight_in_Ginzan_Onsen_town_January_2022_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yamagata Prefecture lies in the south-western corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, and covers 9,325 km2. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita-prefecture">Akita Prefecture</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a> to the east, and Fukushima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a> prefectures to the south. Mountain ranges mark all these boundaries, and most of the population lives in a limited central plain. Yamagata, the capital and largest city, is joined among the major cities by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsuruoka">Tsuruoka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakata">Sakata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a>.</p><p>The climate has long, hot, humid summers and long, snowy winters, with short and rather dry springs and autumns. The central mountains around Gassan may see as much as 3,000 centimetres of snow in a season, while the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakata">Sakata</a> is milder. The prefecture is the largest producer of cherries and pears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ezo people were the aboriginal inhabitants of the area. Together with what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita-prefecture">Akita Prefecture</a> it formed Dewa Province until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. During the Heian period (794–1185) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara family</a> ruled the region. Yamagata city flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1603–1867) as a castle town and post station, known for beni, the red safflower dye used for handspun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. In 1689 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiku">haiku</a> poet Matsuo Bashō visited Yamagata on his five-month journey through the northern regions.</p><p>The area has long been a centre of religious practice. The Dewa Sanzan, three holy mountains, form a traditional pilgrimage for followers of Shugendō, a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>, and the five-storey pagoda at the base of Mount Haguro, the lowest of the three, is a National Treasure. The temple of Yama-dera is carved into the mountainside near Yamagata city. Ginzan Onsen, a hot-spring town in the mountains of Obanazawa, originally grew up around a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yamagata is known for its Hanagasa Festival, held in the city on the first weekend in August, and for a snow lantern festival at the Uesugi Shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a>. Starting in 2003 a project catalogued the Buddhist art of 190 temples in Yamagata city and found a rare seated Vairocana Buddha of zelkova wood at Heisenji Temple, as well as sculpture of the Heian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> periods. Seventeen per cent of the land is designated as Natural Parks, and Mount Zaō is known for its ski slopes and the Okama crater lake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamagata Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
